        "name": "Céa's Lemma",
        "explanation": "Céa's lemma is a fundamental result in the analysis of the finite element method (FEM) for elliptic partial differential equations. It provides a framework for proving error estimates by relating the exact solution of a variational problem to its finite-dimensional approximation. The lemma relies on the properties of Hilbert spaces, bilinear forms, and bounded linear operators. It ensures that the finite element solution converges to the true solution under appropriate conditions, such as continuity and coercivity of the bilinear form.",
        "mathematical_formulation": "Let $ V $ be a Hilbert space with norm $ \\| \\cdot \\| $. For a bilinear form $ a: V \\times V \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R} $ satisfying continuity $ |a(v, w)| \\leq \\gamma \\|v\\| \\|w\\| $ and coercivity $ a(v, v) \\geq \\alpha \\|v\\|^2 $, and a bounded linear functional $ L $, the solution $ u \\in V $ satisfies $ a(u, v) = L(v) $ for all $ v \\in V $. The finite-dimensional approximation $ u_h \\in V_h $ satisfies $ a(u_h, v_h) = L(v_h) $ for all $ v_h \\in V_h $.",
        "source_ids": [
          "wiki_c_a_s_lemma"
        ],
        "concept_id": "7fd32d11-b570-4218-bbb3-e86dfb9342cb"
      }
    ],
